import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a0 as o}from"./chunks/framework.B6reNh8h.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why declarative views","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/why-declarative.md","filePath":"explanation/why-declarative.md"}'),i={name:"explanation/why-declarative.md"};function n(s,e,d,c,l,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="why-declarative-views" tabindex="-1">Why declarative views <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-declarative-views" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why declarative views&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Pavo could have let every product ship its own TUI, its own web app, its own CLI flags. Instead drivers emit a <strong>declarative</strong> tree — data, not code — and hosts interpret it. The reasons:</p><h2 id="one-driver-every-surface" tabindex="-1">One driver, every surface <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-driver-every-surface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One driver, every surface&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <code>ViewNode</code> tree is surface-agnostic JSON. The same <code>myco:board</code> spec renders as an aligned table on the CLI, ratatui widgets in the TUI, and DOM in the web app — with zero per-surface driver code. When a fifth surface appears (a chat bridge, an editor panel), it interprets the same IR.</p><h2 id="drivers-stay-dumb-and-safe" tabindex="-1">Drivers stay dumb and safe <a class="header-anchor" href="#drivers-stay-dumb-and-safe" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Drivers stay dumb and safe&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A driver is a separate process speaking a narrow wire. It can&#39;t inject HTML, run host code, or touch renderer internals — the worst a buggy driver can do is emit a malformed tree, which degrades to KV. That boundary is what lets pavo host <em>untrusted-ish</em> product drivers discovered on <code>PATH</code>.</p><h2 id="degradation-is-a-feature-not-a-fallback" tabindex="-1">Degradation is a feature, not a fallback <a class="header-anchor" href="#degradation-is-a-feature-not-a-fallback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Degradation is a feature, not a fallback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The protocol is <strong>additive-only within v1</strong>: new optional fields, new <code>kind</code> variants, new ops may appear at any time; existing spellings never change meaning. Every consumer obligation follows:</p><ul><li>Renderers meeting an unknown <code>kind</code> degrade to generic KV.</li><li><code>props</code> is an open map — renderers ignore keys they don&#39;t know.</li><li><code>contributes.extra</code> round-trips unknown chrome slots.</li><li>Plugin <code>render</code> failures fall back to built-in interpretation.</li></ul><p>A driver written today keeps working as the IR grows; a renderer written today keeps working as drivers grow. Forward compatibility is mandatory, not optional — it&#39;s what makes &quot;install a driver, get visualization everywhere&quot; true across versions.</p><h2 id="data-binding-not-data-copying" tabindex="-1">Data binding, not data copying <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-binding-not-data-copying" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Data binding, not data copying&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Nodes don&#39;t embed data — they name a <code>query</code> binding, and the host resolves bindings to view-model data before rendering (cached, with partial degradation per binding). Drivers declare <em>what</em> data a view needs; hosts decide <em>when</em> to fetch it. That split is what lets <code>pavo view --param</code> and deep-link params flow through one mechanism, and lets the web surface hydrate panels independently.</p><h2 id="escape-hatches-where-they-belong" tabindex="-1">Escape hatches where they belong <a class="header-anchor" href="#escape-hatches-where-they-belong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Escape hatches where they belong&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Declarative doesn&#39;t mean lowest-common-denominator. <code>embed</code> names a component for rich visuals; <code>renderers[]</code> + the <code>render</code> op let a driver claim a whole kind and answer with a canonical <code>RenderModel</code>; <code>graph</code>&#39;s IR floor covers structure while layout is explicitly the renderer&#39;s problem. The IR covers the 90%; plugins cover the 10% — and fallback is mandatory so plugins can fail softly. See <a href="/pavo/explanation/renderer-plugins.html">Renderer plugins</a>.</p><h2 id="the-cost" tabindex="-1">The cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The cost&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Honest trade-offs: the IR can&#39;t express everything (rich interaction beyond single-form submit — buttons, wizards — is v2), renderer plugins add a second contract to keep honest, and every driver pays the manifest+wire ceremony even for a trivial view. The bet is that uniform surfaces + safe degradation outweigh per-product UI silos — the same bet the org&#39;s other tools (sturn&#39;s WAL, tamias&#39;s ref grammar, cuora&#39;s refs) already make.</p>',16)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
